 {
  "id": "S025",
  "article_id": "gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "…ompanies to build their own semiconductor supply chains.\nDeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng acknowledged in a 2024 interview that chip export restrictions posed a challenge for the company.\nPreviously, DeepSeek used a combination of Nvidia and Huawei chips. The base model supporting DeepSeek R1 was trained using Nvidia's H800 chips, a product specifically designed for the Chinese market before the US government banned its export in late 2023.\nIn recent months, the company has increasingly relied on Huawei's ecosystem. In April, DeepSeek launched its V4 model, which had been optimised for Huawei Ascend chips, while Huawei revealed that training for the V4-Flash model also used Ascend processors.\nThe launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies.\nIf successfully developed, DeepSeek's inference chips would target the fastest-growing segment of the AI industry.\nAs the use of AI applications expands, computing demand is shifting from model training towards inference processes that require more efficient and energy-saving chips.\nHowever, developing AI chips is not an easy task. The semiconductor industry requires significant investment and years of development to produce competitive products.\nAnother challenge comes from US restrictions limiting Chinese companies' access to the most advanced chip manufacturing facilities and high-bandwidth memory (HBM), a key component in modern AI chips.\nAt the same time, DeepSeek has begun opening its…",
  "statement": "The launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies."
 },
 {
  "id": "S026",
  "article_id": "gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce",
  "title": "NVIDIA Unveils New AI Compute Model, But Michael Burry is Shorting Its Stock",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA is expanding its AI infrastructure business with a new model designed to accelerate the deployment of computing capacity across global cloud providers.\nThe move arrives as Michael Burry increases bearish positions against NVIDIA, creating a sharp debate over AI growth prospects.\nNVIDIA (NVDA) Price Performance. Source: TradingView\nNVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce ",
  "passage": "… unlikely to disappear. Any slowdown in spending, infrastructure utilization, or enterprise demand could increase volatility.\nThe result is a high-stakes contest between technological transformation and concerns that expectations may have outpaced economic reality.\nDisclaimer: The content presented above, whether from a third party or not, is considered as general advice only. CFD trading involves significant risk of loss. Past performance does not guarantee future results. This article serves informational purposes only and does not constitute financial advice. Consider your risk tolerance before trading.\nWeekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116. Explore this week’s key market moves, investor concerns and what Australian traders should watch next.\nMeta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116. Explore this week’s key market moves, investor concerns and what Australian traders should watch next.",
