 {
  "id": "S055",
  "article_id": "gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4",
  "title": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of th",
  "passage": "…ts, there is a growing need for computing supply that can scale with usage while maintaining flexibility in payment structures during the shift from pilot projects to full deployment.\nNvidia highlighted the needs of model builders, inference providers, and enterprises requiring immediate cloud access for various AI-related tasks. The company also pointed to AI-native businesses such as Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI as examples of where demand is heading.\nThese entities depend on reliable access to accelerated computing as customer usage increases, often requiring more adaptable commercial arrangements than traditional data center buildouts or fixed infrastructure commitments allow.\nFor Nvidia, this model not only creates a recurring income stream tied to the usage of supported cloud capacity but also deepens its involvement in the AI cloud market as competition intensifies among providers aiming to serve developers, digital-native companies, and established enterprises.\nSharon AI's co-founder and CEO, James Manning, described the agreement as a milestone for the company's plans, stating, \"This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure.\"\nFirmus co-CEO Tim Rosenfield emphasized the importance of infrastructure access for globally competitive AI businesses, noting, \"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally.\"\nHe added that the Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DS…",
  "statement": "For Nvidia, this model not only creates a recurring income stream tied to the usage of supported cloud capacity but also deepens its involvement in the AI cloud market"
 },
 {
  "id": "S056",
  "article_id": "gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96",
  "title": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because",
  "passage": "…tor and the high operational overhead. The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\nA key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\nA Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments, according to Bloomberg . It shows that Nvidia's most ambitious system is taking longer than expected. The current stock weakness is mostly driven by profit-taking. Shawn Oh of NH Investment & Securities pointed to growing uncertainty around Nvidia's expansion plans, which gives alternative AI platforms more room to compete.",
  "statement": "A Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments"
 },
 {
  "id": "S057",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02",
  "title": "Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\nBy Noah Berman\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company wa",
  "passage": "…last June that China could make just 200,000 advanced chips in 2025.\nA Nvidia spokesperson declined to comment on the report. “The ability of China’s domestic ecosystem to yield and scale competitive AI systems is a fact, evidenced by leading Chinese models that are optimized to perform on domestic hardware,” the spokesperson said.\nBut even on Nvidia’s numbers, Huawei would remain way short of the American company’s chip output, let alone being able to “satisfy global demand.”\nIn a report published in December, Chris McGuire , a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations, estimated that if Huawei made 800,000 Ascend 910C chips in 2025 — a figure at the high end of several estimates — that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year.\nMcGuire also estimates that Nvidia’s lead by performance will only grow, even if Huawei were to make 2 million AI chips this year and 4 million in 2027. Industry analysts measure performance by how many calculations a chip can do per second and how fast it can move data around, with Nvidia’s best chips capable of doing far more than those of Huawei.\n“Huawei simply does not pose a competitive threat to Nvidia globally right now,” says McGuire, formerly a national security official who helped design U.S. chip controls. “Huawei’s chips are nowhere near as good, and it can’t make nearly enough of them.”\nIronically, he adds, Nvidia has a lead over Huawei largely because of U.S. export controls …",
  "statement": "that would amount to just 5.3 percent of the total processing power of Nvidia’s chip production that year"
 },
 {
  "id": "S058",
  "article_id": "gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145",
  "title": "Sentiment Could Be Turning Sour on Nvidia. Here’s Where 1 Analyst Thinks NVDA Stock Is Headed Next.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Perhaps the most important stock in the market, Nvidia (NVDA) remains a top stock I continue to watch closely.\nThere are a few reasons for this. Of course, as the world's largest semiconductor company, the fact that Nvidia powers the world's economy (essentially) via propping up the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution is a massive deal. Indeed, the range of opinions on where NVDA stock could be headed from here varies greatly, and we'll get to that shortly.\nDear Netflix Stock Fans, Mark Your",
  "passage": "…t forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors.\nNow, that's not to say that Nvidia's underlying fundamentals don't look strong right now. They do. Looking at the summary above, Nvidia's forward price-earnings multiple of just 35 times is well below the average for the sector. That's important, considering the weighting Nvidia has in most indices (implying the rest of the stock market is, on average, more richly valued than the leader).\nThat said, concerns around a \"circular-financing boom\" put forward by Seaport analysts in their note are one of the issues bears have contended could lead to issues around earnings quality down the line. The thinking is that the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips (either directly via equity or debt, or simply allowing for financing of these increasingly expensive chips), that could mean that the demand investors are seeing isn't real. At least, not to the extent that Nvidia's earnings releases suggest.\nI think there could be something to that. Indeed, the sort of circular activity we saw in the dot-com boom among some tech companies, or in the utility buildout surrounding the rise of the internet, did lead to disaster. And I'm not suggesting Seaport is completely buying …",
  "statement": "concerns around a \"circular-financing boom\" put forward by Seaport analysts in their note"
 },
 {
  "id": "S059",
  "article_id": "gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b",
  "title": "Nvidia underperforms peers as report sparks rack delay worries",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Investing.com -- NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) shares ticked up just 0.8% on Monday, starkly trailing a 3.2% surge in the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index and lagging rivals Advanced Micro Devices Inc (NASDAQ:AMD), which gained 7.7%, and Broadcom Inc (NASDAQ:AVGO), which jumped 4.4%. The underperformance came as a broad rally for chipmakers collided with a boutique research report alleging significant delays in the AI giant's next-generation server architectures.\nSupply chain research firm Se",
  "passage": "Investing.com -- NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) shares ticked up just 0.8% on Monday, starkly trailing a 3.2% surge in the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index and lagging rivals Advanced Micro Devices Inc (NASDAQ:AMD), which gained 7.7%, and Broadcom Inc (NASDAQ:AVGO), which jumped 4.4%. The underperformance came as a broad rally for chipmakers collided with a boutique research report alleging significant delays in the AI giant's next-generation server architectures.\nSupply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane. An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\nThe research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients. Additionally, the report pointed to a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\nThe negative headlines triggered a sharp overnight selloff among Asian tech suppliers, which stumbled on the report as investors locked in profits following massive year-to-date run-ups. Japan's Ibiden Co., a PCB maker that counts Nvidia as its la…",
  "statement": "Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane"
 },
 {
  "id": "S060",
  "article_id": "gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c",
  "title": "TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in last 1 year: 24%\n$NVDA Stock Price as of Jul. 9: $203\n52-Week High: $236\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nTD Cowen reiterated its Buy rating on Nvidia ( NVDA ) stock and named it a Top Pick, maintaining a $275 price target implying roughly 35% upside from where shares closed.\nThe call came after the firm hosted an investor lunch with Nvidia’s leadership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\nAnalyst Joshua ",
  "passage": "…adership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\nAnalyst Joshua Buchalter said Nvidia’s message to investors is clear: computing power remains in short supply, and the market is thinking about Nvidia’s technology far too narrowly.\nHe pointed out that Nvidia’s hardware and software work together, or “co-design,” in a way that gives the company a real edge as AI keeps evolving from general chatbot-style applications toward more specialized, industry-specific tools.\nTo put some numbers behind that, Nvidia estimates about 20% of its business comes from supporting frontier AI models built by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic.\nMeanwhile, TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range.\nThat’s a meaningful chunk of business coming from outside the flashy AI chatbot headlines, which is part of Buchalter’s point about Nvidia’s opportunity being underappreciated.\nTD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices.\nAll of that points to demand outpacing supply.\nNvidia’s own recent quarterly results back up the strength story.\nThe company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\nData center re…",
  "statement": "TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range"
 },
 {
  "id": "S061",
  "article_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd",
  "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams ",
  "passage": "…n Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. The AI buildout and the price of the memory in a laptop are now tied to the same supply squeeze.\nFor enterprise technology buyer…",
  "statement": "First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth."
 },
 {
  "id": "S062",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…t to coal under the banner of “energy security” – especially as surging demand from industries like electronics intensifies.\nNow, Nvidia and the global AI frenzy are locking Taiwan and South Korea deeper into fossil fuel dependency and chronic energy insecurity. The AI boom is fast becoming a carbon trap, and it is the people in East Asia who will be unable to escape worsening pollution and runaway carbon emissions.\nWorse still, while Nvidia quietly drives this environmental destruction in East Asia, it is actively trying to blindfold its own investors. Ahead of its June 24 annual meeting, shareholders proposed that Nvidia report on the lifecycle carbon emissions of its products after sale. The company recommended shareholders vote against the proposal, claiming it is “too prescriptive .”\nNvidia likes to brand itself as a climate champion, but its business model relies on a form of climate colonialism – outsourcing the dirtiest parts of its supply chain to East Asia while actively suppressing transparency.\nIf Jensen Huang truly wants to lead the future, Nvidia must come clean. It must disclose its full lifecycle emissions and legally commit, alongside its Asian manufacturing partners, to producing 100 percent carbon-free AI chips by 2030. Until then, its AI supremacy will remain a dirty empire built on East Asian coal and gas.\nGreenpeace East Asia is part of a global network of independent campaigning organizations dedicated to addressing…",
  "statement": "The company recommended shareholders vote against the proposal"
 },
 {
  "id": "S063",
  "article_id": "gnews:297518a86b6ac724cee70e00",
  "title": "Gradium Raises $100M Seed With NVIDIA Backing",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "CMSWire's Marketing & Customer Experience Leadership channel is the go-to hub for actionable research, editorial and opinion for CMOs, aspiring CMOs and today's customer experience innovators. Our dedicated editorial and research teams focus on bringing you the data and information you need to navigate today's complex customer, organizational and technical landscapes. We've got you covered.\nBy Michelle Hawley\nThe Paris-based voice AI startup extended its seed round seven months after launch and ",
  "passage": "…ou the data and information you need to navigate today's complex customer, organizational and technical landscapes. We've got you covered.\nBy Michelle Hawley\nThe Paris-based voice AI startup extended its seed round seven months after launch and will open a San Francisco Bay Area office.\nFunding milestone reached . Gradium raises total funding to $100 million.\nSilicon Valley expansion . New office in San Francisco Bay to boost AI presence.\nEnterprise developer impact . Increased investment enables faster innovation for enterprise voice AI infrastructure.\nGradium has extended its seed financing to $100 million, seven months after launch. The extension added new investors including NVIDIA, and will fund a new San Francisco Bay Area office.\nThe company was founded in September 2025 by researchers behind Kyutai, an AI research lab known for real-time speech systems. Co-founders Neil Zeghidour, Laurent Mazaré, Olivier Teboul and Alexandre Défossez built Gradium to move frontier voice AI research into production.\nAccording to company officials , the capital will accelerate AI research, product development and international expansion. Gradium said it generated revenue within weeks of launch, though it did not disclose figures.\n\"Surpassing $100 million in funding and expanding our investors marks an important milestone for Gradium. It enables us to accelerate our roadmap, expand our Bay Area presence, and bring years of breakthrou…",
  "statement": "will fund a new San Francisco Bay Area office"
 }
]
